WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.568
-Our next guest is an Emmy
and Tony award-winning

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:05.104
actress you know from shows
such as "Weeds,"

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:06.906
"The West Wing,"
and "Angels in America."

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:08.641
She is starring in
"How I learned to drive,"

00:00:08.641 --> 00:00:10.877
which is playing at the
Manhattan theatre club's

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:12.578
Samuel J. Friedman Theatre.

00:00:12.578 --> 00:00:16.082
Please welcome to the show
Mary-Louise Parker, everyone!

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:18.251
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:27.527 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:36.335 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:36.335 --> 00:00:38.137
How are you?
-I'm great.

00:00:38.137 --> 00:00:39.806
-I'm so happy you're here,
especially --.

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:41.173
-Hi, Syd.
-Yeah, alright, you know

00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:43.176
our bassist, Syd,
very well, right?

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:45.311
-Yeah, his daughter
is a rock star.

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:46.512
She's amazing.
-Yeah.

00:00:46.512 --> 00:00:48.648
-And son, too, but Lyla,
I know better.

00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:51.217
-Alright, well, I hope
Lyla's happy with this, Syd.

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.219
[ Laughter ]

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:55.155
Hey, so, this is really cool
that you're coming tonight,

00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:57.190
because you obviously have a
show this evening on Broadway.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.590
-I do.
-Does this interrupt

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.593
your pre-show warm-up?

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:01.895
-It does.
-Yeah.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:02.961
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.961 --> 00:01:04.264
-This will be my warm-up.

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:07.767
No, I just have a really long
warm-up and, um...

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:09.903
-Have you always had
that for any show?

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:11.270
Okay.
-I have, yeah,

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:12.972
and I'm really superstitious
about it.

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:15.942
And I'm superstitious
about my superstition about it.

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:17.810
-Right, you don't want to talk
about it too much.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.146
but what time --
-I mean, there's no,

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:22.615
like, animals.

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.317
There's no, like, blood.
-Okay.

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:26.085
-Nothing like that,
but it'd just probably

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:27.853
be boring if you saw it.

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:29.788
-Yeah.
-I don't think anybody did think

00:01:29.788 --> 00:01:31.791
it was blood until you said it.

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:33.192
And then it seemed
like a weird thing

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:34.727
you were maybe covering for.
-I promise there's no blood.

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.729
[ Laughter ]

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:38.698
-We were talking about actors
with Andrew, and you know,

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:40.833
the ability to give back
to younger actors.

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.436
You ran a drama camp?

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:44.670
-I did.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.441
Well, it started as just
inviting my kids' friends,

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:51.411
you know, to the country,
to I thought,

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:54.847
well, we'll do plays
and we'll have pie fights

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:57.150
and do some yoga.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.785
And then they invited
their friends,

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:01.654
who invited other friends,
and then it ended up being like,

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:05.158
you know, 18, 20 kids and we put
on a big show at the end.

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.158
It was incredible.

00:02:06.158 --> 00:02:08.261
-And did they all show up

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:10.797
and appreciate what they were
going to have to put into it

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:12.198
to get something out of it?

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:13.532
-No.
No, they did.

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:15.935
Actually, they were incredible.

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:17.603
And I loved them all,
and I see them all.

00:02:17.603 --> 00:02:20.472
Like, to this day, three of them
were at my house

00:02:20.472 --> 00:02:21.674
for game night the other night.

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:23.276
-So you stay in touch
with your drama students?

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:24.711
-Yeah.

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:26.311
You want to come to my camp,
is what I'm feeling right now.

00:02:26.311 --> 00:02:29.381
-I would love -- I feel like --
because I'm a bad actor,

00:02:29.381 --> 00:02:30.783
and I feel like I missed
some early learning.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.684
-But I bet you're
an ideal camper.

00:02:32.684 --> 00:02:34.587
-Oh, I'm a really good camper.
-Yeah, I can see that.

00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:36.723
-As long as you have
super nice bunks.

00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:38.158
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:40.026
-Yeah, bunks are okay,
lots of candy.

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:41.861
-Seth: That's good.
This sounds like a win.

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:46.966
Is your son, do you feel like he
is trending towards acting?

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:48.367
-He is --

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:52.938
I will say he is the best
natural actor I've ever seen.

00:02:52.938 --> 00:02:54.107
-That's very high praise.

00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:56.776
-And my daughter is the best
natural musician.

00:02:56.776 --> 00:02:58.744
I feel like she
just has it in her.

00:02:58.744 --> 00:03:02.815
She picks up any instrument,
and it never makes a bad sound.

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:06.418
-Can I ask, how early did you
know that with her?

00:03:06.418 --> 00:03:10.256
-Well, when she said, you know,
"I'm gonna be playing flute,"

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:11.524
and I thought, you know,
she'd come home

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:13.026
and there would be bad sounds.

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:15.528
It never, ever sounded bad.

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:18.064
-How old was flute?
When did flute start?

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:20.900
-Flute started,
I think she was nine?

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:21.867
-Okay, gotcha.

00:03:21.867 --> 00:03:22.869
-Now she plays harp.

00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:25.971
She plays piano and ukulele.

00:03:25.971 --> 00:03:28.007
-And it's all nice?
-Yeah, all of it.

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:29.541
-That's really great.
-She taught herself.

00:03:29.541 --> 00:03:31.444
It's unbelievable.
And my son is a magician.

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:33.179
-Oh, wow.
-And he's a filmmaker,

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:35.914
but there's a lot --
the magician, I know.

00:03:35.914 --> 00:03:37.516
-Is he good at magic?

00:03:37.516 --> 00:03:41.621
-Well, he made his film
by the money --

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:43.389
he paid for it himself
from the money

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:46.225
he made doing magic lessons.
-Wow!

00:03:46.225 --> 00:03:50.830
So, yeah, he wanted it to be
paid all his money.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:54.433
-So, he taught magic to other
people at a level good enough

00:03:54.433 --> 00:03:56.235
that he financed a film?

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:58.971
-But having a magician for a kid
is like, you know,

00:03:58.971 --> 00:04:02.175
every morning you wake up to,
"Mommy, pick a card."

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:03.375
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.375 --> 00:04:05.144
It's like, "Honey, I'm asleep."

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:08.847
Literally for years,
or there'd be on the couch,

00:04:08.847 --> 00:04:11.584
"What's this bloody thumb?

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:12.718
Why is this here?"

00:04:12.718 --> 00:04:14.787
Like, just things
all over the house.

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:16.122
-Where you might --
-Or he comes out

00:04:16.122 --> 00:04:17.323
coated in blood.

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:18.991
-Does he really?
-No, honest to God.

00:04:18.991 --> 00:04:21.828
-Like, that seems
like a very scary trick.

00:04:21.828 --> 00:04:23.262
[ Laughter ]

00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:26.565
-Yeah, well, I also know all the
best places to buy fake blood.

00:04:26.565 --> 00:04:27.467
This is turning into such
a bizarre...interview

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:29.068
[ Laughter ]

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:31.571
-Now I'm really starting to
think that you

00:04:31.571 --> 00:04:33.706
kill an animal
in your warm-up.

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:37.376
-Alcone in Times Square, you can
get all different colors.

00:04:37.376 --> 00:04:39.979
-Okay, is this a hashtag ad
situation?

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:40.913
Do you work
with the blood people?

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:42.081
-What is that?
-Okay, gotcha.

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:44.716
-I don't know.
-This Broadway show

00:04:44.716 --> 00:04:46.919
is a show you did 25 years ago.

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:50.089
-By the amazing,
phenomenal Paula Vogel.

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:51.490
The play is really special.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:52.625
-And your co-star David Morse

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:54.360
was also your co-star
25 years ago.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.329
-And Johanna Day,
who's extraordinary.

00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:59.399
And David Morse is really...

00:04:59.399 --> 00:05:01.768
He's just some other
level of actor,

00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:05.871
and if you can come
see the play, I mean,

00:05:05.871 --> 00:05:07.073
aspects of it are quite heavy.

00:05:07.073 --> 00:05:09.441
It's about --
I guess it's about --

00:05:09.441 --> 00:05:13.445
Paula says it's about the gifts
from people who hurt us

00:05:13.445 --> 00:05:19.085
and how, you know,
learning to forgive yourself

00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:20.552
because sometimes
we blame ourselves

00:05:20.552 --> 00:05:23.189
when other people have hurt us.

00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:27.926
And it's learning to, uh,
let go of that.

00:05:27.926 --> 00:05:29.394
-Well, that's a very
nice message.

00:05:29.394 --> 00:05:32.064
So 25 years later, you still --
is it true that you

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:33.299
still don't know how to drive?

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:35.734
-No, I'm the worst driver alive.

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:38.470
And I think your producer
fixed my performance,

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:41.740
because not only is it about
driving but I have to mime.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:43.542
I'm a horrible mime.

00:05:43.542 --> 00:05:46.045
I'm driving because, you know,
when you mime,

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:48.948
like, as a steering wheel,
miming,

00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:50.282
then you start talking
and then it becomes

00:05:50.282 --> 00:05:52.251
like a Barbie steering wheel.

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:54.620
It's hard to keep
a consistent feel.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:56.556
Your producer was -- I said,
I was a bad driver

00:05:56.556 --> 00:05:58.157
and a bad mime, and she said,
"Well, you know,

00:05:58.157 --> 00:05:59.825
it's funny because
when I was at the show

00:05:59.825 --> 00:06:02.228
the other day," she said, "You
put the key in the ignition."

00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:04.964
She said, "I think you turned it
towards you."

00:06:04.964 --> 00:06:09.001
And I was on the phone with her,
and I was like,

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:11.404
"Oh, yeah, 'cause that's --
no, 'cause you turn it

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:14.340
this way to turn on a car."

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:17.176
So, last night, I went,
and I was like, "Yay, Sarah,"

00:06:17.176 --> 00:06:21.247
because I --
nobody else noticed.

00:06:21.247 --> 00:06:22.681
Maybe they were too afraid
to tell me.

00:06:22.681 --> 00:06:24.250
-Was that the first time
since the run of the show?

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:28.154
-No, because, one time,
I get in the car,

00:06:28.154 --> 00:06:30.857
I lock the door, I turn the key,

00:06:30.857 --> 00:06:33.425
and I didn't even notice
that I done it.

00:06:33.425 --> 00:06:37.896
When you smile, sometimes people
go, "No," and no one catches it.

00:06:37.896 --> 00:06:39.065
-Well, you know what?

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:40.299
The hope, I think,
for all of us

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:41.600
is that when you do the show
a third time,

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.336
25 years from now,
you get the mime stuff down.

00:06:44.336 --> 00:06:45.470
-Yeah.
-There are really good

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:46.472
mime camps out there.

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:48.040
-Well, I won't be able to.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:49.508
Then I'll be on a walker.

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:50.642
I won't even have
a steering wheel.

00:06:50.642 --> 00:06:52.545
-[ Laughs ] You're going to mime
a Barbie walker?

00:06:52.545 --> 00:06:54.212
[ Laughter ]

00:06:54.212 --> 00:06:55.414
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:55.414 --> 00:06:56.515
-Thank you.
-It's a delight talking to you.

00:06:56.515 --> 00:06:57.950
Mary-Louise Parker, everybody!

00:06:57.950 --> 00:06:59.685
"How I Learned to Drive,"
now playing

00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:01.186
at the Samuel J. Friedman
Theatre.

00:07:01.186 --> 00:07:02.655
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

